  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower and thunderstorm chances shift southward\nin the wake of a cold front today. Slightly cooler and drier\nconditions are forecast for Saturday, before shower and \nthunderstorm chances return on Sunday.\n\nA cold front will move southward across the area this morning. \nAs winds shift to out of the north, slightly cooler and drier \nair will gradually filter in from the north over the course of \nthe day. Dewpoints will drop from the lower 70s currently into\nthe 60s by later this afternoon, and temperatures will still\nclimb into the 80s at lower elevations beneath a mix of sun and\nclouds. As the drier air advects in from the north, instability\nwill be confined to far southwestern portions of the forecast\narea by peak heating this afternoon. As a result, any\nthunderstorms are expected to form across Central Virginia\nbefore eventually tracking off to the south and east out of our\nforecast area. Forecast soundings show less than 1000 J/kg of\nMLCAPE, DCAPE values of 600 J/kg or less, and relatively weak\nwind fields, so any storms that form aren't expected to turn\nsevere. A few showers may be possible further north, but \ncoverage of showers is expected to be isolated at best.\n\nA secondary surge of cold/dry advection will occur tonight\nwithin north to northeasterly flow as high pressure builds to\nour north. Dry conditions are expected across the entire \nforecast area on Saturday within the drier airmass. Temperatures\nare forecast to climb into the 70s to near 80 beneath a mix of \nsun and clouds.\n\nHigh pressure will shift offshore on Sunday, causing winds to\nturn back out of the south. In response, a warmer, more humid \nairmass will work back in from the south and west over the\ncourse of the day, with dewpoints climbing back into the lower\n70s and temperatures reaching into the lower 80s. A deep upper\ntrough will track well to our north across Ontario and Quebec\nduring this time, with an unseasonably strong area of high\npressure building in the zone of subsidence in its wake across\nOntario. This anomalously strong high building to our northwest\nwill encourage a cold front to approach from the north. While\nthe front will likely remain off to our north during the\ndaylight hours, gradually falling heights aloft combined with\nincreasing heat/humidity at low-levels will likely lead to the\ndevelopment of showers and a few thunderstorms Sunday afternoon.\nShowers could then continue on and off through the night as the\ncold front drops southward through the area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily chances for showers persist through much\nof next week, with temperatures likely falling below normal\n\nA cold front drops through the forecast area on Sunday night \nbefore stalling to the south, bringing daily chances for showers.\nIn addition to daily precipitation, a cooler airmass builds \noverhead in the wake of the cold front, yielding cooling \ntemperatures each day. On Monday, high temperatures will be in \nthe 70s for most (60s mtns) with overnight lows in the 50s/60s.\nBy midweek, high temperatures will top out in the upper 50s to \n60s with overnight lows in the 50s. These temperatures remain \nsomewhat uncertain with large model spread, but the forecast \nfavors the cooler side of guidance given a cold air damming \nsurface pattern. Overall, nearby disturbances bring unsettled \nweather to the region persisting through midweek.",
